Induced pluripotent stem cell (iPSC)-derived cell drugs are therapeutic products developed by reprogramming mature cells into pluripotent stem cells and differentiating them into specialized cell types. These technologies offer potential applications in regenerative medicine, neurological disorders, cardiovascular conditions, and cancer immunotherapy.

The market is gaining attention as biotechnology companies and research institutions work to translate stem cell research into clinically applicable treatments. Advances in cell differentiation, manufacturing automation, and quality-control methods are helping researchers improve the consistency and scalability of cell-based therapies. However, high production costs, complex regulatory requirements, and long-term safety considerations continue to influence commercialization.

